  - [1mBrowser-only[0m — a plain [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m at the repo root next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before it. Simplest thing
    that works. Costs: if [38;5;153msrc/[39m ever needs the endpoint, you revisit the format.
  - [1mShared browser + Node[0m — UMD wrapper so both sides can use it. Buys future reuse from [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Costs: boilerplate now
    for a consumer that doesn't exist; [38;5;153msrc/[39m currently holds no config at all.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-only.[0m Nothing in [38;5;153msrc/[39m touches the API today, and YAGNI applies — the upgrade to shared is a
  few lines if it's ever actually needed.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Who consumes the new settings module? → Browser only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question — the shape of what the module exports, since it's the interface [38;5;153mapp.js[39m will use.

  [1mThe fork:[0m today [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a full URL including the [38;5;153m/login[39m path. A settings module can keep that shape (one entry
  per endpoint) or store a per-environment base URL with paths composed at the call site. This matters because it's the
  contract every future caller uses, and changing it later means touching all of them.

  - [1mBase URL + paths[0m — config exposes [38;5;153mapiBaseUrl[39m; [38;5;153mapp.js[39m builds [38;5;153m${apiBaseUrl}/login[39m. Buys: adding a second endpoint is a
    one-line change at the call site, not a new per-env entry in every environment block. Costs: slightly more
    indirection for the single endpoint that exists now.
  - [1mFull URLs per endpoint[0m — config exposes [38;5;153mloginEndpoint[39m directly. Buys: exactly matches today's code, nothing to
    recompose. Costs: every new endpoint multiplies by the number of environments.

  [1mMy recommendation: base URL + paths.[0m The stated goal is making environments easy to change, and a base URL is the
  thing that actually varies per environment — paths don't.
